ling. The area of concern is slightly edematous, but no erythema is apparent. The patient demonstrates a posi- tive Homan sign, and you are concerned that she may have a deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following diagnostic modalities do you order?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI"},{"label":"B","text":"Computed tomographic scanning"},{"label":"C","text":"Venography"},{"label":"D","text":"Real-time ultrasonography Medical and Surgical Complications of Pregnancy Answers"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-16a5c182f895","stem":"Which of the following statements regarding management of labor in a low-risk pregnancy is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amniotomy may shorten the length of labor slightly, but not as much as spon- taneous rupture"},{"label":"B","text":"Universal electronic fetal monitoring improves perinatal outcome"},{"label":"C","text":"Food and oral fluids are acceptable if labor is progressing normally"},{"label":"D","text":"An indwelling catheter is frequently needed when the patient is unable to void spontaneously"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12562a03843d","stem":"Hypertonic dysfunctional labor generally can be expected to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Be associated with rapid cervical dilation"},{"label":"B","text":"Cause little pain"},{"label":"C","text":"Occur in the active phase of labor"},{"label":"D","text":"React favorably to oxytocin stimulation"},{"label":"E","text":"Respond to sedation"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8279aefdc69d","stem":"A forceps rotation of 30° from left occiput anterior (OA) to OA with extraction of the fetus from +2 station would be described as which type of delivery?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outlet forceps"},{"label":"B","text":"Low forceps"},{"label":"C","text":"Midforceps"},{"label":"D","text":"High forceps 129 Copyright © 2004 by the McGraw-Hill Companies, Inc. Click here for Terms of Use. 130 Obstetrics and Gynecology"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["source_attribution_in_option","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f085e8d94342","stem":"Which of the following abnormalities of labor is associated with a significantly increased incidence of neonatal morbidity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged latent phase"},{"label":"B","text":"Protracted descent"},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary arrest of dilation"},{"label":"D","text":"Protracted active-phase dilation"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d604c2ff8395","stem":"Which of the following is most likely to be associated with aspiration pneumonitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting during labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Antacid medications prior to anesthesia"},{"label":"C","text":"Endotracheal intubation"},{"label":"D","text":"Extubation with the patient in the lateral recumbent position with her head lowered"},{"label":"E","text":"Extubation with the patient in the semierect position (semi-Fowler’s)"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4d39f3ebf67e","stem":"Which of the following is the most appropriate management of a face presentation with no fetal distress and an adequate pelvis, as determined by digital examination?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform immediate cesarean section without labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Allow spontaneous labor with vaginal delivery"},{"label":"C","text":"Perform forceps rotation in the second stage of labor to convert mentum poste- rior to mentum anterior and to allow vaginal delivery"},{"label":"D","text":"Allow to labor spontaneously until complete cervical dilation is achieved and then perform an internal podalic version with breech extraction"},{"label":"E","text":"Attempt manual conversion of the face to vertex in the second stage of labor Normal and Abnormal Labor and Delivery 131 Items 187–188 The graph below portrays a labor curve for a woman, gravida 3, para 2, at 39 weeks gestation. Membranes were ruptured at 4 cm. The fluid was clear and there has been no indication of fetal distress. Previous infants weighed 3500 g and 3750 g. The estimated weight of this infant, which appears normal on ultrasound, is 3200 g."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-576eca029af1","stem":"This labor curve is compatible with which of the following conditions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged latent phase"},{"label":"B","text":"Protracted active phase dilation"},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertonic dysfunction"},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary arrest of dilation"},{"label":"E","text":"Primary dysfunction 132 Obstetrics and Gynecology"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"fec6fef76eed6114fb83c7d488bc28383adffd2c"},{"id":"q-c9b2f3e34e52","stem":"In the clinical situation described above, an intrauterine pressure catheter shows a contraction frequency of two per 10 min with an ampli- tude of 35 mmHg. The preferred management is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Gynecology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Gynecology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ambulation"},{"label":"B","text":"Sedation"},{"label":"C","text":"Administration of oxytocin"},{"label":"D","text":"Cesarean section"},{"label":"E","text":"Expectant"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Gynecology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5722f41d64d6","stem":"A primipara is in labor and an episiotomy is about to be cut. Com- pared with a midline episiotomy, an advantage of mediolateral episiot- omy is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ease of repair"},{"label":"B","text":"Fewer breakdowns"},{"label":"C","text":"Less blood loss"},{"label":"D","text":"Less dyspareunia"},{"label":"E","text":"Less extension of the incision"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-93451aa26425","stem":"A 27-year-old woman (gravida 3, para 2) comes to the delivery floor at 37 weeks gestation. She has had no prenatal care. She complains that, on bending down to pick up her 2-year-old child, she experienced sudden, severe back pain that now has persisted for 2 h. Approximately 30 min ago she noted bright red blood coming from her vagina. By the time she arrives at the delivery floor, she is contracting strongly every 3 min; the uterus is quite firm even between contractions. By abdominal palpation, the fetus is vertex with the head deeply engaged. Fetal heart rate is 130/min. The fun- dus is 38 cm above the symphysis. Blood for clotting is drawn, and a clot forms in 4 min. Clotting studies are sent to the laboratory. Which of the fol- lowing actions can wait until the patient is stabilized?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stabilizing maternal circulation"},{"label":"B","text":"Attaching a fetal electronic monitor"},{"label":"C","text":"Inserting an intrauterine pressure catheter"},{"label":"D","text":"Administering oxytocin"},{"label":"E","text":"Preparing for cesarean section Normal and Abnormal Labor and Delivery 133 DIRECTIONS: Each group of questions below consists of lettered options followed by a set of numbered items. For each numbered item, select the one lettered option with which it is most closely associated. Each lettered option may be used once, more than once, or not at all. Items 191–193 For each clinical description, select the most appropriate procedure."},{"label":"A","text":"External version"},{"label":"B","text":"Internal version"},{"label":"C","text":"Midforceps rotation"},{"label":"D","text":"Low transverse cesarean section"},{"label":"E","text":"Classic cesarean section"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"b512ab2ee56fe41a626783a0450550c9e7d71084"},{"id":"q-031414f9d7a9","stem":"A 24-year-old woman (gravida 3, para 2) is at 40 weeks gestation. The fetus is in the transverse lie presentation. (SELECT 1 PROCEDURE) 134 Obstetrics and Gynecology Items 194–196 Select the appropriate treatment for each clinical situation.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidural block"},{"label":"B","text":"Meperidine (Demerol) 100 mg intramuscularly"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxytocin intravenously"},{"label":"D","text":"Midforceps delivery"},{"label":"E","text":"Cesarean section"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1a32634d13dd","stem":"A 59-10 nulliparous woman has had arrest of descent for the last 2 h and arrest of dilation for the last 3 h. The cervix is dilated to 7 cm and the vertex is at 21 station. Monitoring shows a normal pattern and adequate con- tractions. Fetal weight is estimated at 7.5 lb. (SELECT 1 TREATMENT) Items 197–200 Match each description with the most appropriate type of obstetric anesthesia.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paracervical block"},{"label":"B","text":"Pudendal block"},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal block"},{"label":"D","text":"Epidural block"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bfb6a20d0da8","stem":"What is this patient’s most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Latent labor"},{"label":"C","text":"False labor"},{"label":"D","text":"Stage 1 of labor"},{"label":"E","text":"Stage 2 of labor"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"05634f6e4e9ba3bf8af79964ebb2bf1a906c10ce"},{"id":"q-becfb63b0c57","stem":"What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Send her home"},{"label":"B","text":"Admit her for an epidural for pain control"},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture membranes"},{"label":"D","text":"Administer terbutaline"},{"label":"E","text":"Augment labor with Pitocin 136 Obstetrics and Gynecology Items 203–205 A 38-year-old G3P2002 at 40 weeks gestational age presents to labor and delivery complaining of gross rupture of membranes and regular uter- ine contractions every 4 to 5 min. The patient has a history of rapid deliv- eries and was very concerned when she broke her water that she would not make it to the hospital. On arrival to L and D, the patient is in a lot of pain and requesting relief immediately. You check her cervix and note that it is C/5/−2 vertex."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"735ced5e16a81172e6f1f6c076fe3b30b996e543"},{"id":"q-83298a39c25c","stem":"What is the most appropriate method of pain control for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intramuscular Demerol"},{"label":"B","text":"Pudendal block"},{"label":"C","text":"Local block"},{"label":"D","text":"Epidural block"},{"label":"E","text":"General anesthesia"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0f8eb025b325","stem":"Three hours after administering pain relief to your patient, you recheck her cervical exam for progress. Her cervix is unchanged at C/5/−2. You look at the external monitor and note that contractions are occurring every 2 to 3 min. What is the best next step in the management of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Gynecology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Gynecology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Place a fetal scalp electrode"},{"label":"B","text":"Rebolus the patient’s epidural"},{"label":"C","text":"Place an intrauterine pressure catheter (IUPC)"},{"label":"D","text":"Prepare for a cesarean section secondary to a diagnosis of secondary arrest of labor"},{"label":"E","text":"Administer Pitocin for augmentation of labor"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Gynecology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-04bf53839969","stem":"After placement of an IUPC, you calculate that the patient has 280 Montevideo units. You continue to allow the patient to labor, and after two more hours you reexamine the patient and find that her cervix is unchanged. What is the best next step in the management of this patient?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform a cesarean section"},{"label":"B","text":"Continue to wait and observe the patient"},{"label":"C","text":"Augment labor with Pitocin"},{"label":"D","text":"Attempt delivery via vacuum extraction"},{"label":"E","text":"Perform an operative delivery with forceps Normal and Abnormal Labor and Delivery 137"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c3efaf040a54","stem":"A 29-year-old G2P1 in active labor at 41 weeks, with cervix C/5/0 vertex, has just received an epidural for pain control. She is on Pitocin because her uterine contractions had spaced out to every 10 min. The nurse calls you because the fetal heart rate has been in the 70s for the last 3 min. All of the following are appropriate next steps except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prep for emergent cesarean section"},{"label":"B","text":"Check BP"},{"label":"C","text":"Discontinue Pitocin"},{"label":"D","text":"Administer oxygen"},{"label":"E","text":"Turn the patient on her left side"},{"label":"F","text":"Check the patient’s cervix Items 207–208 You are delivering a 26-year-old G3P2002 at 40 weeks. She has a his- tory of two previous uncomplicated vaginal deliveries and has had no com- plications this pregnancy. After 15 min of pushing, the baby’s head delivers spontaneously, but then retracts back against the perineum. As you apply gently downward traction to the head, the baby’s anterior shoulder fails to deliver."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b2ddc9745cf","stem":"All of the following are appropriate next steps in the management of this patient except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Instruct the nurse to apply fundal pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"Cut a generous episiotomy"},{"label":"C","text":"Instruct the nurse to apply suprapubic pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"Instruct the nurse to flex the patient’s legs back to her head"},{"label":"E","text":"Call for help"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-266a2fcb3f00","stem":"The baby finally delivers and the pediatricians attending the delivery note that the right arm is hanging limply to the baby’s side with the forearm extended and internally rotated. What is the baby’s most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erb palsy"},{"label":"B","text":"Klumpke’s paralysis"},{"label":"C","text":"Humeral fracture"},{"label":"D","text":"Clavicular fracture 138 Obstetrics and Gynecology Items 209–212 A 41-year-old G1P0 at 39 weeks, who has been completely dilated and pushing for 3 h, has an epidural in place and remains undelivered. She is exhausted and crying and tells you that she can no longer push. Her tem- perature is 101°F. The fetal heart rate is in the 190s with decreased vari- ability. The patient’s membranes have been ruptured for over 24 h, and she has been receiving intravenous ampicillin for a history of colonization with group B strep bacteria. The patient’s cervix is completely dilated and effaced and the fetal head is in the direct OA position and is visible at the introitus between pushes. Extensive caput is noted, but the fetal bones are at the +3 station."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9f347bdf60d2","stem":"What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deliver the patient by cesarean section"},{"label":"B","text":"Encourage the patient to continue to push after a short rest"},{"label":"C","text":"Attempt operative delivery with forceps"},{"label":"D","text":"Rebolus the patient’s epidural"},{"label":"E","text":"Cut a fourth-degree episiotomy"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"735ced5e16a81172e6f1f6c076fe3b30b996e543"},{"id":"q-a02a761e5571","stem":"Compared to the use of the vacuum extractor, forceps are associated with an increased risk of which of the following neonatal complications?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalohematoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Retinal hemorrhage"},{"label":"C","text":"Jaundice"},{"label":"D","text":"Intracranial hemorrhage"},{"label":"E","text":"Corneal abrasions"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d5031bec0009","stem":"What kind of forceps would be most appropriate to use in this deliv- ery?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kielland"},{"label":"B","text":"Piper"},{"label":"C","text":"Simpson Normal and Abnormal Labor and Delivery 139"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8026fc08f878","stem":"During the delivery, it is necessary to cut an episiotomy. The tear extends through the sphincter of the rectum, but the rectal mucosa is intact. How would you classify this type of episiotomy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"First-degree"},{"label":"B","text":"Second-degree"},{"label":"C","text":"Third-degree"},{"label":"D","text":"Fourth-degree"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ab653f63fea7","stem":"A 16-year-old G1P0 at 38 weeks gestation comes to the labor and delivery suite for the second time during the same weekend that you are on call. She initially presented to L and D at 2:00 P.M. Saturday afternoon complaining of regular uterine contractions. Her cervix was 50/1/−1 ver- tex, and she was sent home after walking for 2 h in the hospital without any cervical change. It is now Sunday night at 8:00 P.M., and the patient returns to L and D with increasing pain. She is exhausted because she did not sleep the night before because her contractions kept waking her up. The patient is placed on the external fetal monitor. Her contractions are occurring every 2 to 3 min. You reexamine the patient and determine that her cervix is unchanged. What is the best next step in the management of this patient?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform artificial rupture of membranes to initiate labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Administer an epidural"},{"label":"C","text":"Administer Pitocin to augment labor"},{"label":"D","text":"Achieve cervical ripening with prostaglandin gel"},{"label":"E","text":"Administer 10 mg intramuscular morphine"},{"label":"F","text":"Perform a cesarean section 140 Obstetrics and Gynecology Items 214–215 A 25-year-old G1P0 patient at 41 weeks presents to labor and delivery complaining of gross rupture of membranes and painful uterine contrac- tions every 2 to 3 min. On digital exam, her cervix is C/3 with fetal feet pal- pable through the cervix. The fetus’s estimated weight is about 6 lb, and the fetal heart rate tracing is reactive."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1171f91e9d98","stem":"What is the best method to achieve delivery?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Labor and Delivery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Labor and Delivery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deliver the fetus vaginally by breech extraction"},{"label":"B","text":"Deliver the baby vaginally after external cephalic version"},{"label":"C","text":"Perform an emergent cesarean section"},{"label":"D","t